==========================================
CLOUD/DEVOPS DAY 03 NOTES — IAM Policies, Roles, Least Privilege
Date: 25-11-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- A policy's Action + Resource together define exactly what's allowed - narrow is safer
- "Action": "*", "Resource": "*" is the textbook overly-permissive red flag
- A custom policy (create-policy + attach) is different from attaching an AWS-managed policy ARN
- An IAM role is assumed (by a service, another account, or a user), not logged into directly like a user
- A role's trust policy (Principal) controls WHO is allowed to assume it - that's the real access control
- Least privilege = only the specific actions + specific resources actually needed, nothing broader

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] awslocal iam create-policy --policy-document file://least-privilege-policy.json
[ ] awslocal iam create-user + attach-user-policy (least privilege)
[ ] awslocal iam create-policy --policy-document file://overly-permissive-policy.json
[ ] awslocal iam create-user + attach-user-policy (overly permissive)
[ ] awslocal iam create-role --assume-role-policy-document file://trust-policy.json

paste your real output here :
$ awslocal iam list-attached-user-policies --user-name day03-least-priv-user
> 

$ awslocal iam list-roles
